  <h1>Custom software or off-the-shelf: how to decide</h1>
  <p class="lead">When a ready-made product is the smart choice, and when building something tailored pays off.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-layout">
  <article class="prose article" data-article>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px; border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border: 1px solid var(--border); background: #0f172a;">
      <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/blogs/custom-software-vs-off-the-shelf.svg') }}" alt="Custom software or off-the-shelf: how to decide" style="width: 100%; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; display: block;">
    </div>
    <p>&quot;Should we build or buy?&quot; is one of the most common questions we hear. The honest answer is that both are right in different situations.</p>
<h2 id="when-off-the-shelf-software-is-the-better-choice">When off-the-shelf software is the better choice</h2>
<p>If your process is standard and a well-supported product covers most of what you need, buy it. You get faster setup, ongoing updates and a vendor to support it. Accounting, email and basic project management are usually good examples.</p>
<h2 id="when-custom-software-starts-to-make-sense">When custom software starts to make sense</h2>
<p>Consider building when:</p>
<ul><li>Your process is a real source of advantage and generic tools would flatten it.</li><li>You are paying for many licences but using a small part of the features.</li><li>You are running the business through workarounds, spreadsheets and manual copying between tools.</li><li>You need several systems to work together in a way no single product supports.</li></ul>
<h2 id="the-hidden-costs-on-both-sides">The hidden costs on both sides</h2>
<p>Off-the-shelf tools can bring per-user fees that grow with your team, limits on customisation and data that is hard to move. Custom software brings upfront cost, a need for maintenance and a dependency on whoever built it. Plan for these honestly.</p>
<h2 id="a-middle-path">A middle path</h2>
<p>Many businesses combine the two: a standard product for commodity needs, plus custom software or integrations for the parts that make them different. This often delivers most of the benefit at a fraction of the risk.</p>
<h2 id="questions-to-ask-before-deciding">Questions to ask before deciding</h2>
<ol><li>How closely does a product match our actual process today?</li><li>What do we pay over three years, including fees, workarounds and staff time?</li><li>How hard would it be to leave this product later?</li><li>Do we have a plan for maintaining custom software?</li></ol>
<p>If you are unsure, we are happy to review your situation and tell you plainly whether building makes sense. You can <a href="/contact">talk to us about your project</a> at any stage.</p></article>
